    "notes": "- Free verse / lack of punctuation: mimics natural speech, reflecting the spontaneity and elusive quality of happiness.\n- Line breaks and enjambment: create pauses and surprises, mirroring the fleeting, unexpected arrival of happiness.\n- Conversational tone via direct address to the reader: makes happiness seem ordinary, found in everyday moments rather than grand experiences.\n- First-person perspective: personalises the reflection on happiness, showing its fragility when clouded by adult concerns.\n- Symbol of the coconut: represents happiness as something mysterious, simple, and contained, yet difficult to access.\n- Child imagery (small hands, beaming face): innocence and wonder show happiness as pure curiosity and delight.\n- Contrasting imagery (hard, hairy, swishing vs. beaming, pleading): highlights the tension between adult detachment and childlike joy.\n- Repetition of “because”: mirrors adult rationalisations that deny happiness, showing how logic can suppress joy.\n- Repetition of “happiness”: emphasises its centrality and elusive quality, almost like the speaker is trying to grasp it through words.\n- Adult vs. child perspectives: contrasts practicality with wonder, suggesting happiness is more easily embraced through a child’s eyes.\n- Want vs. need: reveals how happiness doesn’t fit neatly into necessity; it thrives in desire and curiosity.\n- Plain diction: reinforces the ordinariness of happiness, found in everyday encounters rather than elevated or abstract language.\n- Alliteration / soft sounds (“something swishing”): captures the sensory appeal of the coconut and the moment of discovery.",
