    "notes": "- Fragmented syntax / short sentences: mirror the turbulence and volatility of emotions in the marriage.\n- Juxtaposition of diction ‘hate’ and ‘love’: shows the shifting, contradictory nature of emotions within a long-term relationship.\n- First-person interior monologue interpolated into third person narrative (“I should have left him then, Delia thought”): allows access to Delia’s private, conflicted thoughts while retaining narrative distance.\n- Domestic imagery (bedroom, sheets, chewing, noises, body): highlights intimacy but also irritation, capturing how small details breed resentment.\n- Ocean/shore imagery: reflects emotional flux; the sea becomes a metaphor for both escape and constancy.\n- House as symbol: represents endurance and indifference, suggesting relationships are fragile compared to time and place.\n- Nature imagery (grasses, sand, daisies, gulls, rust, salt): conveys resilience, decay, and impermanence, paralleling the shifting states of love and resentment.\n- Matter-of-fact tone (“It was a marriage”): normalises conflict as inherent to relationships.\n- Irony (“Not sorry enough”): captures bitterness and unresolved emotional tension.\n- Repetition of “hated” and “sorry”: underscores cycles of resentment and reconciliation.\n- Contrasting registers (“Her husband’s penis, tucked decorously…” vs. “all this shit together”): blends bluntness with lyricism, showing the rawness and complexity of emotions.\n- Personification of the house (“There will be others after you… Your troubles mean nothing”): suggests the smallness of individual struggles against a larger, indifferent world – paradoxically, a source of resilience.\n- Hatred metaphorised as a wound (“Her hatred scabbed over”): makes emotion tangible, showing how time dulls but does not erase pain\n- Sensory detail (breathing, chewing, “unknown woman on his breath”): makes resentment visceral and embodied.",
