    "criteria": "3 – Explains effectively how an idea about the human capacity for curiosity is represented in this image\n2 – Explains how an idea about the human capacity for curiosity is represented in this image\n1 – Provides some relevant information about how an idea about the human capacity for curiosity is represented in this image",
    "notes": "- The boy is the focal point on the right, lying on his stomach, holding a magnifying glass to his eye so that his eye appears hugely enlarged; the intense gaze is directed at a tiny creature on his open palm.\n- Tools of investigation (binoculars, a microscope in a plastic bag, a net, spades, a bucket and a tin box) show curiosity as a systematic, active exploration.\n- Contrast with the adults in the background, relaxed under an umbrella and absorbed in reading, implies curiosity belongs to those who look closely at small things.\n- Composition: items on the towel lead the eye along a line to the boy; a wide panoramic frame with a bright, uncluttered beach and pale blue sky.\n- Colour: soft pastel blues and whites with saturated primary accents (red and blue spades, yellow shirt, green bucket) make discovery joyful.\n- The sandcastle on the far right and the open sea suggest wider worlds still to explore.",
    "model": "Wiesner represents curiosity as an active, absorbing human drive. The boy is the focal point on the right, lying on his stomach and holding a magnifying glass to his eye, which magnifies it enormously, so his intense gaze at the tiny creature on his palm dominates the image. Around him lie tools of investigation, binoculars, a microscope in a bag, a net and spades, suggesting curiosity leads to a systematic exploration of the world. In contrast, the adults sit relaxed in the background, absorbed in reading, implying that curiosity belongs to those who look closely at small wonders. The bright, uncluttered beach setting makes discovery seem joyful and open.",
